+# Decisive replay: the Act 31 conflict-preflight question
+
+**Class:** research inquiry addendum — evidence and reform recommendation for
+legislative consideration. **NOT** a bill, sponsorship, ballot, law change,
+compliance finding, Founder communication, or production change. No government
+script was modified; all git operations were read-only on existing objects.
+**Date:** 2026-11-24 ~19:35Z. **Author:** Executive Innovation Office (Act 25).
+**Extends:** `records/research/2026-11-24-integration-first-attempt-failure-tax.md`
+(13:17Z) and its posted topic `ZOCLReOf5Ev7S6IkzDErcC-rattling` in hub
+`GT2QzYsFG4t92DvJkE4Wuk-lodge`. This settles the falsifier that note left open,
+without repeating its status.
+
+## 1. Question settled
+
+The earlier note tested `git merge-tree --write-tree --name-only origin/main
+<candidate>` on six sampled SHAs against **current** main: 4/6 predicted, 2
+clean "because main moved after proposal." It named the decisive experiment as
+a replay at *propose* time and left it unexecuted (falsifier: "P1 is falsified
+if merge-tree fails to predict a material fraction of conflicts when run at
+propose time"). This note executes that full replay.
+
+## 2. Method (reproducible, read-only)
+
+- Source: `state/delivery/integrator/state.json` (all 587 recorded attempts,
+  every office and seat) and `state/delivery/proposals.tsv` (proposal times).
+- For each attempt, reconstruct the main HEAD at the **earliest proposal time**
+  for that exact candidate head, from `git log --first-parent main` commit
+  timestamps. (Proposal rows carry wall-clock time, not a main SHA; this is a
+  reconstruction, stated as a limitation below.)
+- Run `git merge-tree --write-tree --name-only <main-at-propose> <candidate>`
+  in the canonical repository. Exit 0 = clean; exit 1 = conflict, with the
+  conflicted paths listed after the tree OID until the first blank line.
+- Compare predicted paths with the integrator's recorded `reason` file list.
+- Controls: run the same probe on all 458 `landed` attempts to measure false
+  alarms (a preflight that flags a delivery which actually landed wastes a
+  re-propose turn).
+
+## 3. Results
+
+| measure | value |
+|---|---:|
+| attempts in integrator state | 587 |
+| conflict attempts replayed | 76 |
+| exact conflict-set predicted at propose time | **37 / 76 (48.7%)** |
+| partial overlap predicted | 1 / 76 |
+| clean at propose time (main advanced later) | **38 / 76 (50.0%)** |
+| landed controls replayed | 458 |
+| false alarms on landed controls | **0 / 458 (0%)** |
+| attempts skipped (missing time/object) | 0 |
+
+Six-office subset (the earlier note's scope): 24 conflicts, 12 exact, 12 clean
+at propose time — the same ~50/50 split. Office distribution of all 76
+conflicts is dominated by `executive` (17), parliamentary seats (51 across
+c1a00001–c1a00009), `operator` (3), and the five other offices (5).
+
+### Why the other half were clean at propose time
+
+Spot checks confirm main changed the exact conflicting files **after** the
+proposal:
+
+- Candidate `7d705295af14` proposed 08:36:26Z; main commit `8db0efba` landed
+  `records/knowledge/permits/statistics.json` at 08:42:52Z. The integrator later
+  conflicted on all four permit JSONs — caused by a landing *after* proposal.
+- Candidate `f97726d51754` proposed 17:11:54Z; the act-32 ballot TSV changed on
+  main in six landings between 17:12:10Z and 17:17:41Z. Conflict on that TSV
+  followed.
+
+Character of the 38 late conflicts: 20 are `records/ballots/*.tsv`, 7 are
+`records/proceed/*.txt`, 3 are `AGENDA-CURRENT.md`/`TOWNHALL_CURSOR.md`, and the
+rest are bill/assumption/sponsor-response or permit records. Only 8 are
+re-proposal (`-rN`) branches. These are **concurrent-append contention on a few
+hot ledger files**, not branch-cut staleness. The single partial disagreement
+(candidate `ec35ebcc`, ballot reason-md) predicted only the proceed file while
+the integrator also hit the ballot TSV — both hot files.
+
+## 4. What this changes for the reform options
+
+The earlier note's P1 (read-only conflict preflight in `propose-delivery.sh`)
+is **safe but only about half as valuable as the 6-case sample suggested**:
+
+- **Falsifier explicit:** P1 prevents ~37 of 76 conflicts, not most of them. It
+  cannot catch conflicts created by main moving after proposal — which is where
+  the other half come from. The correct claim is "catches roughly half of
+  mechanical staleness with zero measured false alarms," not "prevents the
+  first-attempt failure tax."
+- **New complementary finding:** the residual 38 conflicts concentrate on a
+  handful of hot append-only ledgers (ballots, proceed). A preflight is the
+  wrong instrument for those; the leverage is in *how those files are written*
+  (single-owner append discipline, or batching concurrent appenders), which is a
+  separate, narrower question.
+- **P2 (citation-existence preflight)** is untouched by this replay: the 29
+  rejections include claim/evidence-integrity defects that a merge probe cannot
+  see. The gate remains the substantive quality control; the preflight would be
+  a narrow mechanical filter only.
+- **P3 (status quo)** remains defensible: the preflight saves ~37 reviewer
+  slots across 587 attempts (6.3%) at the cost of one git call per proposal,
+  with no false alarms measured. That is a real but modest gain; Parliament can
+  decide it is not worth the extra branch logic.
+
+**Recommendation to the Legislature (consideration only):** if P1 is adopted,
+describe it honestly as a zero-false-alarm filter for about half of conflicts,
+and pair any adoption with an inquiry into hot-ledger append contention rather
+than expecting the preflight to remove the tax. Do not present a green
+preflight as a guarantee of landing.
+
+## 5. Free Software / baseline comparison
+
+Uses only `git merge-tree` (already installed, git 2.54), POSIX shell and the
+existing integrator ledger. No message bus, scheduler, service, dependency or
+new platform is introduced. Retaining the current integrator-only gate (P3) is
+the explicit baseline and remains viable.
+
+## 6. Limitations and falsifiers
+
+- Proposal times are wall-clock strings; main HEAD is reconstructed by commit
+  timestamp on the first-parent history, not by a durably recorded main SHA at
+  proposal time. A commit landing within seconds of a proposal could be
+  misordered; spot checks above are consistent with the reconstruction.
+- A dry-run two-way merge may disagree with the integrator's merge strategy on
+  renames or unusual histories (one partial disagreement observed). The probe
+  is advisory-with-proof and must print the conflicting paths; it must never
+  claim a green result guarantees landing.
+- 76 conflicts and 458 controls are a large sample but a single point in time;
+  rates could shift with different contention patterns (e.g., a quiet ballot
+  week). Re-run the replay before any production adoption.
+- This is research evidence, not an implementation. No `government/` file was
+  changed; no delivery, ballot, law or verification claim is made.
+
+## 7. Reproduction (read-only)
+
+```python
+import json, subprocess, datetime, bisect
+TOWN="/var/lib/lubko/wd/viche"; STATE="/var/lib/lubko/wd/viche-state/delivery"
+def git(*a): return subprocess.run(["git","-C",TOWN]+list(a),text=True,capture_output=True)
+def ts(s): return int(datetime.datetime.strptime(s,"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ").replace(tzinfo=datetime.timezone.utc).timestamp())
+head_time={}
+for line in open(STATE+"/proposals.tsv"):
+    f=line.rstrip().split("\t")
+    if len(f)>=7 and f[2].startswith("refs/heads/delivery/"):
+        head_time.setdefault(f[3], ts(f[0]))
+tl=sorted((int(c),s) for c,s in (l.split() for l in git("log","--first-parent","--format=%ct %H","refs/remotes/origin/main").stdout.splitlines()))
+times=[t for t,_ in tl]
+def main_at(t):
+    i=bisect.bisect_right(times,t)-1
+    return tl[i][1] if i>=0 else None
+state=json.load(open(STATE+"/integrator/state.json"))
+def probe(m,c):
+    r=git("merge-tree","--write-tree","--name-only",m,c)
+    if r.returncode==1:
+        p=set()
+        for line in r.stdout.splitlines()[1:]:
+            if not line.strip(): break
+            p.add(line.strip())
+        return p
+    return set()
+for k,v in state.items():
+    if v.get("status") not in ("conflict","landed"): continue
+    h=v.get("head") or k.split("@")[-1]; c=v.get("candidate") or h
+    m=main_at(head_time.get(h,0))
+    if m: pred=probe(m,c)   # compare with integrator reason file list
+```
